# LoRaWAN compliance — US915 Class A

Airtime is shared and TTN is free infrastructure someone else pays for. Citation keys resolve
in `docs/CITATIONS.md`.

**Fixed parameters** (`FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §3–4; changing any needs an ADR): US915, **Class A
only** (Class C holds the receiver open — incompatible with the power budget), OTAA, ADR ON,
confirmed uplink **OFF**, interval default 3600 s within 900–86400 s inclusive. The 60 s bench
cadence exists only in builds with `FEATURE_RADIO=0` and must never reach the air.

## Class A consequences

A Class A device is reachable **only** in RX1/RX2 right after its own uplink [CIT-LW-LINK]:

1. A downlink cannot arrive on demand — never write logic that waits on one.
2. Do not sleep, reconfigure RS-485, or start Modbus between TX and the close of RX2; the cycle
   is poll → TX → RX → sleep (§5).
3. Silence after an uplink is the normal case, not an error.

## Airtime budget

TTN Fair Use is ~30 s uplink airtime and ~10 downlinks per device per day [CIT-TTN-FUP]; that
is what sets the 900 s interval floor (§4). At 900 s: ~36 s/day at DR0 (**over**) and ~6 s at
DR3 (well under) — compliant at DR3+, marginal at DR0. If a deployed node is observed at DR0,
raise its interval. On TTN US915, uplinks run SF7–SF10 BW125 on 903.9–905.3 MHz, RX2 is
923.3 MHz SF12BW500 [CIT-TTN-FREQ]; payload size is smallest at the lowest DR [CIT-LORA-RP002],
so a payload that only fits at high DR silently fails at the coverage edge. The stack enforces
FCC dwell limits [CIT-FCC-15247]; do not defeat them by pinning a channel.

- **Never enable confirmed uplinks** — each forces a downlink.
- **Never retry a failed uplink immediately** — bounded exponential backoff only (H4); the node
  must survive multi-day gateway outages without an airtime death spiral.
- **Join must back off and sleep between attempts** — a tight join loop hammers the gateway
  and never sleeps, flattening the battery in days [CIT-RAK-SLEEP]. See rule 50.

## Downlink handling — validate, then persist

Opcode-led on fPort 10 (§4): `0x01` set-interval carries uint32 BE `interval_s` (5 bytes),
`0x03` request-status (1 byte). Check fPort → opcode → length → range 900–86400; anything wrong
is **ignored and the previous value kept** — never reset to a default, never accept out of
range. Persist to flash only when the value changed, never during brownout (H3, H5), apply on
the next wake.

## Uplink content

Nulls stay null — a failed read never encodes `0` (§2.1); a zero wind speed and a dead
anemometer must never be indistinguishable. One failed sensor does not suppress the uplink:
send what was read, with validity flags (§5). Channel and type IDs are contract — rule 60.
